          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will the sealer change the polish or make it shiny?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A high-quality impregnating sealer (like the non-enhancing ones on this list) works entirely below the surface. After you wipe off the excess, there is no film left behind. The physical finish of the marble—whether honed, leathered, or highly polished—remains exactly as it was. It provides zero added shine. If you want more shine, you need a physical polish (mechanical abrasion), not a chemical sealer. This is fundamentally different from how concrete countertop sealers work, where topical film-forming products are standard.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "I accidentally let the sealer dry on top and it's hazy. How do I fix it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "This is called \"sealer residue.\" The simplest solvent for dried sealer is often the wet sealer itself. Apply a generous amount of fresh sealer over the hazy spot and vigorously scrub with a clean microfiber cloth; the wet solvents re-activate the dried polymers. If that fails, carefully wipe the area with a small amount of acetone or mineral spirits, immediately buffing dry. Do not let pure solvents pool on the counter, as they can travel to the stone's edges and damage nearby adhesives.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a sealer prevent the marble from cracking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Sealers are chemical barriers for liquids, not structural reinforcements. Cracking in marble is caused by sub-surface settling, thermal shock (placing a frozen turkey and a hot pot next to each other), or \"blow-outs\" from heavy impacts. A sealer protects the color and health of the crystals, but it does not add any flexural strength to the slab itself. The same principle applies to cinder block sealers and basement wall sealers — they prevent moisture damage but cannot fix structural issues.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is there any sealer that stops acid etching?",
