            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Short answer: No \"invisible\" penetrating sealer can stop etching. Long answer: There are specialized \"stone protective films\" (similar to a screen protector for a phone) that can be professionally applied over the marble. These are made of high-grade polyester or polyurethane and physically block the acid. However, these films are expensive to install, have a limited lifespan, and can alter the tactile \"cold\" feel of the stone that many people love.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my marble look darker after sealing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If you used a standard (non-enhancing) sealer and the stone looks significantly darker, it usually means the sealer has not yet fully evaporated. Solvent carriers can stay trapped for 24-48 hours, keeping the refractive index of the stone altered. If it's still dark after a week, it's possible you didn't wipe off the excess residue properly, or you accidentally used an \"enhancer\" without realizing it. On rare occasions, certain white marbles react with solvent carriers to create a \"greying\" effect, which is why testing a small inconspicuous area first is paramount.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
