                <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
                    Marble is unequivocally one of the most stunning building materials on earth. From sweeping Carrara marble countertops to elegant Calacatta Gold bathroom floors, it instantly elevates a room. But its geological makeup—primarily compressed calcium carbonate—means it is both softer and more porous than granite or quartz. Without a professional-grade sealer, your pristine white marble will inevitably surrender to olive oil, red wine, and coffee.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The natural stone industry is flooded with conflicting advice about marble maintenance. Some recommend cheap topical acrylic sealers (which will peel), others suggest completely ignoring it (which guarantees ruin). The truth is determined by chemistry: marble possesses a network of microscopic capillary pores. When a highly pigmented or oily liquid touches an unsealed slab, capillary action pulls the liquid deep beneath the surface. Once the liquid evaporates, the pigment or oil remains permanently bound within the stone matrix. 
                </p>

                <p>
                    The purpose of a proper marble sealer is not to create a physical sheet of plastic over the stone, but to fundamentally alter the surface tension of the stone's microscopic pores, repelling liquids from entering in the first place. You don't want a "coating"—you want an invisible, internal shield.
                </p>

                <h2 id="staining-vs-etching" class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    The Crucial Distinction: Staining vs. Etching
                </h2>

                <p>
                    Before reviewing products, we must address the most common misconception in the natural stone industry. Understanding the difference between <em>staining</em> and <em>etching</em> will save you immense frustration and false expectations.
                </p>

                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 my-10">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-red-100">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-red-800 mt-0 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
                            <svg class="w-6 h-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 11H5m14 0a2 2 0 012 2v6a2 2 0 01-2 2H5a2 2 0 01-2-2v-6a2 2 0 012-2m14 0V9a2 2 0 00-2-2M5 11V9a2 2 0 002-2m0 0V5a2 2 0 012-2h6a2 2 0 012 2v2M7 7h10"></path></svg>
                            Staining
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0">
                            <strong>What it is:</strong> Liquid (wine, oil, coffee) entering the pores of the stone and leaving pigment behind, causing dark or colored spots.<br><br>
                            <strong>Can sealers prevent it?</strong> <strong>YES.</strong> A high-quality impregnating sealer blocks stains entirely by reducing the stone's absorption rate. Oil and water will bead on the surface for hours, allowing you time to wipe them away.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-orange-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-orange-100">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-orange-800 mt-0 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
