                <!-- Application Guide -->
                <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-16 rounded-r-2xl shadow-sm">
                    <h2 id="the-master-application-guide-how" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-6">
                        The Master Application Guide: How to Seal Marble Properly
                    </h2>
                    
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6">
                        Applying an impregnating sealer is not like painting. The goal is to flood the pores of the stone, wait just long enough for absorption, and entirely remove the excess from the surface before it dries.
                    </p>

                    <div class="space-y-6">
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                                Step 1: Deep Clean & Dry (The 24-Hour Rule)
                            </h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">
                                Clean the marble thoroughly using a dedicated pH-neutral stone cleaner (never vinegar, bleach, or acidic all-purpose cleaners). If the marble is a kitchen countertop, use acetone on a clean rag to remove invisible grease barriers. Most importantly, the stone must be <strong>bone dry</strong>. If you sealed a shower, wait 24–48 hours after the last use so moisture deep in the pores evaporates. Water trapped in pores will block the sealer from entering.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                                Step 2: Flood the Surface
                            </h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">
                                Pour the sealer onto the marble or apply via a clean sprayer. Spread it evenly using a lamb's wool applicator, a pristine microfiber cloth, or a high-density foam brush. Do not apply a thin coat like paint—you want to <strong>puddle</strong> the liquid. The surface should look completely wet and glistening.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                                Step 3: Dwell Time (Watch the Absorption)
                            </h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">
                                Let the sealer sit wet on the surface for 5 to 10 minutes (consult manufacturer directions). Pay attention to any dull spots that appear empty—this means the stone is highly porous in that area and has sucked the sealer in. Add more sealer immediately to keep those areas wet. <em>Never let the sealer dry on the surface during this phase.</em>
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold flex items-center gap-2 text-red-700 mb-2">
                                <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z"></path></svg>
                                Step 4: The Crucial Wipe Off
                            </h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">
                                This is where 90% of amateur failures occur. After the dwell time, before the liquid dries, you must vigorously wipe the surface completely dry with fresh, lint-free microfiber cloths. You are physically removing 100% of the sealer from the surface. The only sealer doing any work is the sealer <em>inside</em> the pores. If you leave a microscopically thin film on top, it will dry into a hazy, sticky mess that requires heavy solvent to strip. Rub the marble until it feels dry to the touch and the natural polish shines clearly.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                                Step 5: The Sub-Surface Cure
                            </h3>
                            <p class="text-gray-600 m-0 text-sm">
                                While it feels dry immediately, the polymers inside the pores undergo a chemical cross-linking phase. Keep water and objects off the counter for at least 24 hours. Wait 72 hours before assessing true stain resistance. If the marble is exceptionally porous, apply a second coat 1-2 hours after the first wipe-down.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 id="the-kitchen-survival-test-what" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    The Kitchen Survival Test: What Actually Damages Sealed Marble?
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Even with a "bulletproof" sealer, your marble is not invincible. It is a biological and chemical entity. Here is a breakdown of common household substances and how they interact with sealed stone:
                </p>
                <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
                    <div class="p-6 bg-red-50 rounded-2xl border border-red-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Category A: The Acid Attackers</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
                            <strong>Agents:</strong> Lemon juice, balsamic vinegar, pickle juice, soda, tomato sauce.<br>
                            <strong>The Danger:</strong> Etching. Even the best sealers cannot stop the chemical reaction between acid and calcium. Acid will dissolve the surface of the stone, creating a dull "etch" mark in seconds. Wiping immediately is the only defense.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="p-6 bg-blue-50 rounded-2xl border border-blue-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Category B: The Oil Infiltrators</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
                            <strong>Agents:</strong> Olive oil, bacon grease, coconut oil, butter, peanut butter.<br>
                            <strong>The Danger:</strong> Long-term staining. A quality sealer buys you hours of time. On unsealed stone, oil wicks in instantly. On sealed stone, it stays on top. However, if left for 12+ hours, oil can slowly navigate past the sealer's polymers.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="p-6 bg-purple-50 rounded-2xl border border-purple-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Category C: The Pigment Powerhouses</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
                            <strong>Agents:</strong> Red wine, turmeric, beet juice, blueberries, pomegranate.<br>
                            <strong>The Danger:</strong> Deep discoloration. Pigments are microscopic particles. If the carrier liquid carries them into the pores, they "stain" the crystal walls. Sealers are 95% effective here, but "stain-proof" is a marketing term; they are technically "highly stain-resistant."
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="p-6 bg-green-50 rounded-2xl border border-green-100">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Category D: The Chemical Killers</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
                            <strong>Agents:</strong> Bleach, Windex, abrasive scouring powders, oven cleaner.<br>
                            <strong>The Danger:</strong> Sealer Degradation. Harsh chemicals strip the polymers out of the pores. Using a non-neutral cleaner effectively "un-seals" your stone, leaving it vulnerable to Categories A, B, and C.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 id="the-ultimate-marble-maintenance-schedule" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    The Ultimate Marble Maintenance Schedule
                </h2>
                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                        <div class="bg-[#1E90FF] text-white font-black p-2 rounded-lg text-xs">DAILY</div>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Wipe surfaces with a pH-neutral stone cleaner (like Granite Gold Daily Cleaner) and a microfiber cloth. Dry the surface immediately—don't let water pool around faucets, as minerals in tap water can cause "calcification" build-up over time.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                        <div class="bg-gray-800 text-white font-black p-2 rounded-lg text-xs">MONTHLY</div>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Check for etching and minor surface wear. If you find dull spots, use a marble polishing paste to gently restore the shine. Apply a "spray-and-wipe" maintenance sealer to high-traffic areas (around the sink and stove) to maintain the repellent barrier.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                        <div class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black p-2 rounded-lg text-xs">YEARLY</div>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Perform the <strong>Water Drop Test</strong>. If water soaks into the stone in under 10 minutes, clear the counters and perform a full deep-clean with acetone, followed by a professional-grade saturation of Tenax Proseal or Dry-Treat. This is the only way to ensure another year of worry-free living.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

