                <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Frequently Asked Questions
                </h2>
                
                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my marble needs sealing?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Perform the water drop test. Drip a tablespoon of water onto the most heavily used area of the marble (usually near the sink) and wait 15 minutes. If the water beads up into a tight sphere like rain on a freshly waxed car, your sealer is intact. If the water flattens out and the marble underneath turns dark, the stone is absorbing the moisture and must be resealed immediately. Note: Check several areas, as sealer wears out faster on "work zones" compared to decorative corners.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">My countertop is already stained. Can I seal over it?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Never seal over an active stain. The sealer will lock the stain inside the pores permanently, making removal virtually impossible. Treat existing stains using a specialized poultice powder mixed with hydrogen peroxide or a commercial stain remover designed for marble. The poultice draws the stain out over 24-48 hours. Once the stone is clear and dry, then apply the sealer. For oily stains, a "solvent poultice" using acetone and baking soda is often required to break down the surface tension of the grease.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Will the sealer change the polish or make it shiny?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">A high-quality impregnating sealer (like the non-enhancing ones on this list) works entirely below the surface. After you wipe off the excess, there is no film left behind. The physical finish of the marble—whether honed, leathered, or highly polished—remains exactly as it was. It provides zero added shine. If you want more shine, you need a physical polish (mechanical abrasion), not a chemical sealer. This is fundamentally different from how <a href="/best-concrete-countertop-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">concrete countertop sealers</a> work, where topical film-forming products are standard.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">I accidentally let the sealer dry on top and it's hazy. How do I fix it?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">This is called "sealer residue." The simplest solvent for dried sealer is often the wet sealer itself. Apply a generous amount of fresh sealer over the hazy spot and vigorously scrub with a clean microfiber cloth; the wet solvents re-activate the dried polymers. If that fails, carefully wipe the area with a small amount of acetone or mineral spirits, immediately buffing dry. Do not let pure solvents pool on the counter, as they can travel to the stone's edges and damage nearby adhesives.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a sealer prevent the marble from cracking?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">No. Sealers are chemical barriers for liquids, not structural reinforcements. Cracking in marble is caused by sub-surface settling, thermal shock (placing a frozen turkey and a hot pot next to each other), or "blow-outs" from heavy impacts. A sealer protects the color and health of the crystals, but it does not add any flexural strength to the slab itself. The same principle applies to <a href="/best-cinder-block-sealer-reviews/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">cinder block sealers</a> and <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">basement wall sealers</a> — they prevent moisture damage but cannot fix structural issues.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there any sealer that stops acid etching?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Short answer: No "invisible" penetrating sealer can stop etching. Long answer: There are specialized "stone protective films" (similar to a screen protector for a phone) that can be professionally applied over the marble. These are made of high-grade polyester or polyurethane and physically block the acid. However, these films are expensive to install, have a limited lifespan, and can alter the tactile "cold" feel of the stone that many people love.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my marble look darker after sealing?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">If you used a standard (non-enhancing) sealer and the stone looks significantly darker, it usually means the sealer has not yet fully evaporated. Solvent carriers can stay trapped for 24-48 hours, keeping the refractive index of the stone altered. If it's still dark after a week, it's possible you didn't wipe off the excess residue properly, or you accidentally used an "enhancer" without realizing it. On rare occasions, certain white marbles react with solvent carriers to create a "greying" effect, which is why testing a small inconspicuous area first is paramount.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="mt-16 border-t border-gray-100 pt-12">
                    <h3 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Related Sealer Guides</h3>
