                <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Marble Sealing Mistakes
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    Marble is calcium carbonate, which means acid dissolves it. Understanding what a sealer can and cannot prevent is most of the battle.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Sealer to Prevent Etching</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Sealers resist staining — liquid soaking into the stone — and do nothing about etching, which is acid chemically dissolving the polished surface. Lemon juice, wine, and vinegar will leave a dull mark on sealed marble just as they do on unsealed. The sealer buys you time to wipe up, not immunity.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an Enhancing Sealer Without Testing</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Colour-enhancing sealers deepen the stone permanently, and on marble they can turn a bright white slab distinctly grey or yellow. Always test on an offcut or an inconspicuous corner and let it dry fully before committing to the whole surface.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Excess Sealer to Dry on the Surface</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Impregnating sealers are meant to soak in, and whatever has not absorbed within the stated dwell time has to be buffed off. Left to dry, it cures as a hazy, streaky film that has to be removed with solvent. Watch the clock and wipe the surface back with a clean cloth.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Acidic or Abrasive Cleaners Afterwards</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Vinegar, bathroom cleaners, and most general-purpose sprays are acidic enough to etch marble and strip a sealer in the process. Once the stone is sealed, maintain it with a pH-neutral stone cleaner only — everything else shortens the life of both the sealer and the polish.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Closing -->
                <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
                  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    The best <strong class="text-white">marble sealer</strong> for most installations is a quality impregnating sealer that soaks in, repels water and oil, and leaves the stone looking exactly as it did. Enhancing sealers deepen the colour and suit honed or tumbled marble, but they change the appearance permanently, so test before committing.
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                    Whatever you use, remember what sealing does and does not do: it buys time against stains, and it offers no protection at all against acid etching. Wipe spills promptly, clean with a pH-neutral product only, and re-seal when water stops beading on the surface.
                  </p>
                </div>

